      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To observe longer term (9 to 18 months) follow up of women after a transcervical resection of the endometrium (TCRE) and the effect of post-operative medroxyprogesterone acetate on the results."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "An observational study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "A district public hospital and a nearby private hospital in Sydney, Australia."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SUBJECTS",
          "content": "Sixty-one women who underwent TCRE from January to December 1991 were contacted 9 to 18 months after the procedure. They were questioned about symptoms and levels of satisfaction at six months, 12 months and at the time of interview, 9-18 months after the procedure. The 27 women treated from 1 January to 31 August 1991 were given medroxyprogesterone acetate 150 mg at the time of surgery. From 1 September to 31 December 1991 no medroxyprogesterone acetate was given (32 women). Two women underwent immediate hysterectomy, and are included as unsuccessful TCREs but are excluded from description of effects of medroxyprogesterone acetate on the results of TCRE."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Late onset, defined as after 12 months, of pain with or without bleeding occurred in 7 out of 52 women (13.5%). Overall, 49 out of 61 women (80.3%) were satisfied with the operation at the time of interview, 9 to 18 months post-operatively. Success rates, measured by satisfaction, were 89% (24/27) in the medroxyprogesterone acetate group and 75% (24/32) in the no medroxyprogesterone acetate group."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Follow up after endometrial resection revealed that a definite subgroup of women develop late onset of pain with or without bleeding. Results at four to six months do not necessarily correlate with longer term outcomes. This needs to be investigated with larger, long term follow up studies, as does the apparently beneficial effects of medroxyprogesterone acetate on the long term results of TCRE."
        }
      ]},
